<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Oriskany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1777">1777</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Oriskany was fought on 6 August 1777 near Oriskany in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. A Patriot militia column under Nicholas Herkimer, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a> allies, marching to relieve Fort Stanwix was ambushed by Loyalists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> warriors. One of the bloodiest engagements of the war, it set members of the Iroquois Confederacy against each other.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-oriskany.jpg" alt="Battle of Oriskany" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frederick Coffay Yohn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Herkimer_at_the_Battle_of_Oriskany%22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In June 1777 the British Army launched a two-pronged attack from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> under General John Burgoyne, aiming to split <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> from the other colonies by taking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-valley">Hudson Valley</a>. While Burgoyne came south across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-champlain">Lake Champlain</a>, Brigadier General Barry St. Leger led about 1,800 regulars, Jäger, Loyalists of the Royal Yorkers and the British Indian Department, and Indigenous warriors down the Mohawk Valley. His force besieged Fort Stanwix, a Continental Army outpost, from 2 August. Nicholas Herkimer, head of Tryon County's Committee of Safety, raised about 800 militiamen, mostly poorly trained farmers of Palatine German descent, joined by 60 to 100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a> warriors under Han Yerry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Warned by a message from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molly-brant">Molly Brant</a> to her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a>, St. Leger sent Sir John Johnson's men and about 400 warriors and rangers to intercept the column. Herkimer wished to wait for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> signal from the fort, but his captains accused him of being a Tory, and he marched on. About 10 km from the fort the road dipped into a marshy ravine, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> chiefs Sayenqueraghta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornplanter">Cornplanter</a> laid the ambush.</p><p>At about 10 am the warriors opened fire too early, yet the Patriots were taken by surprise. Herkimer's leg was shattered; propped against a beech tree, he kept directing his men. The rear of the column fled, and within 30 minutes only about half his men were still fighting. After a thunderstorm halted the battle for an hour, Herkimer had his men fight in pairs. Meanwhile Lieutenant Colonel Marinus Willett led 250 men out of the fort and looted the nearly deserted enemy camps, and the warriors withdrew to protect them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Americans lost around 465 dead, wounded or captured, Johnson's force 93. Herkimer's leg was amputated and he died on 16 August. Discontent among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s Indigenous allies, who had done most of the fighting and lost belongings in the sortie, helped St. Leger's expedition fail: when Benedict Arnold's relief column approached, the besiegers withdrew.</p><p>The battle began a war within the Haudenosaunee Confederacy. Brant's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawks</a> burned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a> settlement of Oriska, and the Oneidas plundered the Mohawk strongholds of Tiononderoge and Canajoharie. Haudenosaunee oral histories call the site A Place of Great Sadness. A monument went up in 1883, and the battlefield became a National Historic Landmark in 1962.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Oriskany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Mohawk warriors under Hendrick Theyanoguin fought beside the British at the Battle of Lake George in 1755, and in the American Revolution the league’s nations fought one another, notably at the <b>Battle of Oriskany</b> in 1777.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the American Revolution the Oneida fought for the Americans at <b>Oriskany</b> and sent corn to Valley Forge, yet lost most of their land through New York State treaties.</blockquote><a href="/state/oneida-nation">Oneida Nation</a></li></ul></section>
